# {{ .ProviderShortName }} Provider

{{ .Description }}

With it you can describe a [Pangolin](https://github.com/fosrl/pangolin) deployment as code: organizations, sites, public resources and their targets, private resources, access rules, roles, users and API keys.

## Getting started

### 1. Requirements

- Terraform 1.0 or later, or OpenTofu 1.6 or later.
- A Pangolin instance (self-hosted or cloud) with the [Integration API](https://docs.pangolin.net/manage/integration-api) enabled. The provider talks to this API, not to the web UI.
- The provider is tested against Pangolin 1.24. Some attributes need a recent server and say so in their description (for example `METHOD` rules and `response_headers_json` need 1.24).

### 2. Create an API key

In the Pangolin dashboard, create an API key and grant it the actions your configuration needs. A root (server admin) key can manage organizations; an organization key is limited to one organization. The secret is shown once.

### 3. Configure the provider

```terraform
{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}
```

`endpoint` is the base URL of the Integration API **including the version prefix**, for example `https://api.pangolin.example.com/v1`. Both settings can come from the environment instead, which keeps secrets out of your configuration:

```shell
export PANGOLIN_ENDPOINT="https://api.pangolin.example.com/v1"
export PANGOLIN_API_KEY="..."
```

### 4. Publish your first service

This exposes a service running behind a site at `https://app.example.com`, and blocks write requests (POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) from everywhere except your office network.

```terraform
resource "pangolin_organization" "main" {
  org_id         = "acme"
  name           = "Acme"
  subnet         = "100.90.128.0/24"
  utility_subnet = "100.96.128.0/24"
}

resource "pangolin_site" "home" {
  org_id = pangolin_organization.main.org_id
  name   = "home-lab"
  type   = "newt"
}

resource "pangolin_domain" "main" {
  org_id      = pangolin_organization.main.org_id
  type        = "wildcard"
  base_domain = "example.com"
}

resource "pangolin_resource" "app" {
  org_id      = pangolin_organization.main.org_id
  name        = "app"
  mode        = "http"
  domain_id   = pangolin_domain.main.domain_id
  subdomain   = "app"
  apply_rules = true
}

resource "pangolin_target" "app" {
  resource_id = pangolin_resource.app.resource_id
  site_id     = pangolin_site.home.site_id
  ip          = "192.168.1.10"
  port        = 8080
}

resource "pangolin_resource_rule" "office" {
  resource_id = pangolin_resource.app.resource_id
  action      = "ACCEPT"
  match       = "CIDR"
  value       = "203.0.113.0/24"
  priority    = 1
}

resource "pangolin_resource_rule" "no_writes" {
  resource_id = pangolin_resource.app.resource_id
  action      = "DROP"
  match       = "METHOD"
  value       = "POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE"
  priority    = 2
}

# The site connector (Newt) needs these to connect.
output "newt_credentials" {
  value     = { id = pangolin_site.home.newt_id, secret = pangolin_site.home.secret }
  sensitive = true
}
```

Run `terraform init && terraform apply`, then start Newt on the home-lab machine with the credentials from `terraform output -json newt_credentials`.

### 5. Adopt what you already have

Most resources support `terraform import`; the exact ID format is at the bottom of each resource page, for example `terraform import pangolin_resource.app acme:5`.

## What the provider manages

| Area | Resources |
|---|---|
| Organization | `pangolin_organization`, `pangolin_domain` |
| Connectivity | `pangolin_site` |
| Public resources | `pangolin_resource`, `pangolin_target`, `pangolin_resource_rule` |
| Private resources | `pangolin_private_resource` |
| Access | `pangolin_role`, `pangolin_user`, `pangolin_role_resource_grant`, `pangolin_user_resource_grant` |
| Automation | `pangolin_api_key` |
| AI gateway | `pangolin_ai_provider` |

Data sources exist for organizations, domains, sites, resources, roles and users.

## Known limitations

- **Internal (password-based) users are not supported.** The Integration API rejects creating them, so `pangolin_user` only manages users from an OIDC identity provider.
- **Removing a role from a user** (shrinking `role_ids` on `pangolin_user`) uses a route that exists only in Pangolin's commercial edition, and fails with a clear error on the community build.
- Raw `tcp`/`udp` resources need the `allow_raw_resources` server flag, and SSH sudo command or group lists on roles need a license with role-based SSH controls. Each resource page notes its own caveats.
- `org_id` on the provider is currently informational; every resource takes its own `org_id`.

